WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:07.410
David, I like the idea of single payer, but
how do you deal with increased demand for

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:13.730
medical services if single payer passes, if
everything is free at the point of care, people

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:18.070
are going to start going to doctors for all
sorts of procedures.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.810
How do we deal with that?

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.009
This is sort of a ruse, OK?

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:26.180
And I like the I love the opportunity to talk
about this because there's so many different

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.570
things to consider.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.759
First of all, let's apply the sniff test to
it.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:37.750
If you're talking about unnecessary medical
procedures where people would get them for

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:44.080
kicks if it was free, what actually falls
under that umbrella that has no preventive

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.080
benefit?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:51.950
What I mean by that is in every single payer
proposal, I've seen unnecessary cosmetic plastic

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:52.950
surgery.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:53.950
That's not medically necessary.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.310
It's not reconstructive in some way, for example,
wouldn't be covered by single payer.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.940
You would still have to say, if I want breast
implants, if I want a nose job absent some

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:06.110
trauma or, you know, a mastectomy or other
situations, it's not covered.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.720
I've got to pay for that out of pocket.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:12.420
OK, so once you take cosmetic plastic surgery
off the table.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.670
What what somebody who people are going to
get hip replacements just for kicks, even

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:23.440
though they don't really need them, people
are going to get, you know, dental cleanings

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.420
which reduce dental cleanings, reduce the
cost of care by preventing cavities as an

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.370
example and preventing gum issues.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:37.300
So when you really get down to it, you could
see a decrease in demand for some of the more

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.050
resource intensive health care.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:47.500
Preventive care reduces the number of E.R.
visits for heart attacks and strokes and abscesses

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:54.450
that could have been treated early on and
reduces demand for ICU and other acute services.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.180
Because you're catching medical issues earlier,
you're addressing them earlier, and you prevent

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.100
some of that really expensive, specialized
care.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:08.590
So no one, when you really think about it,
the things people are mostly not doing because

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:13.760
of financial obstacles aren't things people
do for fun.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.440
And this gets to the most important point.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.480
And really the fundamental reason why a for
profit health care system doesn't make sense.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.310
Demand for most medical services is completely
inelastic.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:31.970
OK, let's talk this through, and I hope I
can do this clearly going back to my microeconomic

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:32.970
days.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:40.209
If you take a good look, for example, I don't
know Sharpies as an example where there's

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:48.810
lots of substitutes for Sharpies and Sharpies
are an item where you might prefer the Sharpie

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.750
to a pen, but you might not.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.810
But there's subsidies, OK, etc..

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.909
If Sharpies are a penny, each demand explodes
tons of people.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:01.999
If the Sharpie goes to a penny, I'll buy a
ton of sharpies and just use them, maybe instead

00:03:01.999 --> 00:03:03.830
of a pen or some other marker.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:08.700
If the Sharpie goes to a thousand dollars,
demand goes to almost zero.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.279
Very few people like Sharpies enough to pay
a grand each and they'll switch to something

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:13.279
else.

00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:14.969
You'll go to a pencil, you'll go to, you know,
whatever.

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:18.499
If there's no obvious alternatives, you'll
you'll you'll get Chalke.

00:03:18.499 --> 00:03:19.499
I don't know.

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:21.159
OK, so that's there's elasticity there.

00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:23.370
Significant elasticity.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:29.350
When you take if you fall down and you need
a hip replacement, whether the hip replacement

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:35.170
costs a million dollars or a hundred thousand
dollars or twenty five thousand dollars or

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:41.510
ten thousand dollars or a thousand dollars,
my demand for a hip replacement is absolute.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:47.910
If I need a hip replacement and it's essentially
zero if I don't need a hip replacement, no

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:53.260
matter how cheap you make hip replacements,
no matter how much access you give me to hip

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.260
replacements with single payer.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:58.489
And of course, it ignores that a doctor wouldn't
recommend one if there's no reason to do it.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:00.299
My demand for a hip replacement is zero.

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:02.560
So this is not a normal market.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.560
Good.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:08.809
And so the reality is, you know, consider
appendectomy.

00:04:08.809 --> 00:04:14.949
I have I had an appendectomy a year and a
few months ago, up until the day on which

00:04:14.949 --> 00:04:22.550
my appendix started swelling and requiring
to come out, my demand for appendectomy services

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:23.759
was zero.

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:24.759
Regardless of the cost.

00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:26.150
I wouldn't even take a free appendectomy.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.300
I wouldn't even take money to get an appendectomy.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.460
Suddenly I need one and my demand is absolute.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.430
OK, so this is the point.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:41.440
The increase that people are expecting is
simply not going to be there from the standpoint

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.240
of people getting services they don't need.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.069
Most of the services people would avail themselves
of if money were not an object are going to

00:04:49.069 --> 00:04:53.490
be preventive services that actually reduce
acute disease in the long run and reduce costs.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.659
So it's essentially a farce.

00:04:55.659 --> 00:04:57.759
That's the reality.

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:00.330
And one last thing I know some people will
say.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:08.020
But, David, when access to Medicaid is increased,
emergency room visits go up very, very different.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.680
Medicaid is not Medicare.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.310
Medicaid is primarily for poor or disabled
folks.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:21.919
And in particular, it's folks who have a backlog
of more serious chronic issues than the average

00:05:21.919 --> 00:05:23.930
population.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.319
And it is not representative of what would
happen.

00:05:27.319 --> 00:05:33.470
Bigger picture you have, there's very many
specific issues with that that make it not

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:34.950
analogous to what you would see.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:35.950
Bigger picture.

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:36.950
All right.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:37.722
So let me know if I'm missing anything, but
that's my general take on that.

